Original commit body from a5b3d53d7cf6cbd9e57045d2994d72397b00010c:

io_boundary._module_matches normalised :: and / to . and then asked
`cm in em or em in cm` — bidirectional, case-insensitive SUBSTRING containment.
25 of the 210 catalog sink modules are four characters or fewer (os, io, fs,
net, log, sys, rpc, ssl …), so each matched ANY module whose normalised path
merely contained it. Found by the 2026-08-01 taint cohort over 9 fresh external
repos — the run that moved INV-karud to violated — which reported d3's `log`,
THE LOGARITHM FUNCTION, as a logging sink, and net/http/httptest.NewRequest,
the TEST request constructor that performs no network IO, as a network sink.

Matching is now component-aware, with a different rule per direction:

  * exact / component-PREFIX. Where the prefix is strict, the first EXTRA
    component decides: capitalised means a type inside the matched module
    (os/exec + Cmd), lowercase means a sibling or sub-package (net/http +
    fcgi, httptest, smtp) and must not match.
  * component-SUFFIX, unrestricted — dropped qualification. This is how source
    actually spells these: Go writes `http.Get` after importing "net/http",
    Java writes System.in for java.lang.System.in. Leading namespace cannot
    turn one module into a different one the way an appended sub-package can.
  * Swift receiver-variable carve-out, where the DIRECTION is the safety
    property: the catalog name may end with the hint, never the reverse.
    'chaos'.endswith('os') is the bug; 'NonBlockingFileIO'.endswith('fileIO')
    is the feature. Bounded further to suffixes starting on a capital so it
    cannot degenerate back into substring containment for short names.

THE OBVIOUS FIX DOES NOT WORK, and an existing test said so. Keeping / distinct
from . cleanly separates net/http.Client (a type) from net/http/fcgi (a
package) — except the Go analyzer emits os.exec.Cmd for what the catalog spells
os/exec, so the separator is not evidence of a package boundary. A second
existing test then caught an over-tightening: java.lang.System against a hint of
`System` is a component SUFFIX, not a prefix. Both regressions were caught by
tests that predate this change, which is why the whole io-boundary and taint
suite was run rather than only the new class.

EVIDENCE. The item's own filed repro re-runs clean: all five verified-live
collisions, all four fresh-substrate observations, and the one catalog-internal
pair that flips a ZONE rather than a primitive (sys.process.Process is host_fs,
sys.process.ProcessBuilder is subprocess, and the former is literally a
substring of the latter). 3436 tests pass; changed source at 100% coverage. The
self-tree validation ratchet is clean, so the shrink-only re-baseline the item
anticipated was not needed.

Self-tree impact is NIL and that was measured, not assumed: an A/B with the
analysis cache cleared and a POSITIVE CONTROL on each arm — asserting the loaded
predicate's verdict on os/chaos before measuring — gives 735 boundary calls both
ways with a byte-identical whole output. Expected rather than suspicious: every
observed defect was Go or JavaScript and this tree is Python, which is exactly
why INV-karud refused to close on self-tree numbers.

A first attempt at that A/B was DISCARDED as untrustworthy. It compared 735 to
735 with its only validity check a grep for "cm in em or em in cm" — a string
present both in the old code and in the new docstring that quotes it, so it
could not distinguish the arms. Two identical numbers from an instrument that
cannot tell the conditions apart is a stale read, not a null result.

KNOWN TRADEOFF, in the docstring rather than left to be discovered: a lowercase
extra component now blocks a match even in languages that do not signal types by
case, so this can UNDER-match where it previously over-matched. For a sink
catalog that is the safe direction — a missed sink is a gap, a spurious one is a
false claim about the program's behaviour. The fresh-substrate cohort is the
place that gets measured; this item is tagged awaits_bakeoff_validation because
no self-tree number can validate it.

